training your rotational strength  training in the transverse plane should  be non-negotiable this is movement an  entire plane in real life you don't only  move in the frontal or the sagittal  plane you need to twist your body  whether you're throwing a ball or  javelin throwing a punch or a kick or  grappling rotation is extremely  important  however a lot of training programs don't  include anything for rotation if you're  following a conventional bodybuilding  program or powerlifting program there's  a good chance you aren't training your  rotational strength at all and if you  are you might not be doing enough so  today i'm going to talk you through a  bunch of different rotational exercises  you can do and why they're so important  and as it would happen somebody has left  some bands here attached to this tree  which could come in handy  [Music]  and once again today i'm joined by grant  stephens what's up guys he's going to  help me get some more dynamic shots do  some training and if you are interested  in training and martial arts and  lifestyle then check out his channel  i'll put a link in the description down  below loads of really awesome stuff  there if you are a little bit of out of  the curve and you're already doing some  training for your rotational strength  then chuck hang on wait a second  better so if you are a bit ahead of the  curve and you're ready to do some  training for your rotational strength  then chances are one of the moves you  might be using is the pilot press if  you're not familiar with it essentially  what you're going to do is take a band  or a cable you're going to step a little  bit away so there's some tension  and then you're going to hold it close  to your body like so facing in the same  direction and then push it outwards from  your body basically what happens here is  that as the band as the  handle gets further away from your body  it increases the torque and what you  have to do is fight the rotational force  which is trying to twist you that way  that makes this an anti-rotation  movement  about core stability  this becomes really useful if you're  trying to prevent someone from twisting  you to the ground or just performing a  bunch of different calisthenics  movements like a one-armed push-up  obviously you need to train it on both  sides like so  and this is a really useful tool to  include in your arsenal  so another great movement for  anti-rotational stability is the  one-armed push-up this is a slightly  more difficult  option and here basically what you're  doing is you're trying to push yourself  up so you get all the benefits of a  push-up you're putting more weight onto  one side so it's a great way to load one  peck and one tricep more but at the same  time trying to prevent gravity from  pulling that other shoulder down towards  the ground and twisting your body so it  is an anti-rotation movement  when you perform this you want to make  sure you've got your shoulder depressed  as much as possible  lower to the ground  and push up  so now you might already be doing those  movements and if that's all you're doing  for your rotational strength and it's  certainly better than nothing and it is  a useful thing that should definitely  include however it's not enough on its  own you can't just do a couple of  exercises for your entire plane of  motion i think that's going to cover  everything it's like saying well i'm  doing squats that's all i need for the  sagittal plane it just doesn't work like  that so when you perform movements like  this the very nature of an anti-rotation  movement is isometric so you're only  strengthening yourself in that one  position with a little bit of spillover  but there's no range of motion you need  some fully twisting movements as well if  you're gonna be able to develop strength  from every angle and really tap into as  much power as possible in those  positions this is where some other  movements come in and some other things  you might already be doing include  things like russian twists and twisting  sit-ups these are also great exercises  and you should of course be doing them  but they have their limitations as well  first of all there's not that much  resistance they're not that explosive so  you're building strength endurance and  improving your proprioception but you're  not building a lot of strength and  explosiveness in those ranges at the  same time you're only rotating your core  because rotation doesn't just mean core  rotation rotating your waist it also  means hips when you rotate your hips and  your core together that's when you  develop real power when you throw a  punch when you throw a ball when you're  moving you need to be able to remove  these things at the same time and you  need to be able to ground yourself and  drive yourself off the ground to develop  that power so we need to train this but  i'm not saying that russian twists  aren't useful because being able to also  just move your upper torso is useful in  order to learn that proprioception a lot  of us are very tight we don't rotate  much at all in our daily lives and as  such we forget how to move those parts  of our body independently you might have  tight ql muscles for example which can  limit movement in this range and it's  also just feeling yourself moving in  that area and so obviously you might  also want to do some targeted mobility  work for the thoracic spine rotation in  particular and you can do something here  like the grab reach i've talked about  before basically just get into the crab  position but then reach over and behind  that's perfect because you're getting in  that rotation but also some lateral  flexion for the spine and some extension  as well  so like i say you need to be able to  rotate the core on its own but you also  need to be able to take the core with  the hips to really drive power through  your body if you were throwing a hook  for example you wouldn't twist just your  upper body you'd be able to develop  barely any power that way you want to  throw the hips in as well and the same  thing goes if you're throwing a ball  throwing a regular cross or really  engaging in a whole range of motions  fortunately there's lots of ways that we  can train this too let's say when you're  punching and kicking for example you  need to rotate through the hips and  through the core and that's actually a  great way to practice this i've said  this before even if you're not planning  on being a fighter not interested in  martial arts throwing punches and kicks  and doing shadow boxing and heavy bag  work is a fantastic workout not just for  the metcon but also for proprioception  for building rotational strength and  explosiveness so i highly recommend you  incorporate this in your training at the  same time there's a bunch of other  things you can do for example one of my  favorites is the medical slam so you've  got the rotational slam you've got the  shot put through anything that involves  twisting the body as you throw the ball  and you're going to drive through and  really build that ballistic power this  also works the serape effect which i've  talked about in the past which is the  kind of sling of muscles that you engage  when you use movements like a throw and  it's one of the most powerful movements  that the human body can perform so it's  a fantastic thing to be training  so when you're throwing the med ball  like that you want to really focus on  not just generating power through your  core but through your hips as well  really talking the hips and pushing  through the foot in the same manner  another way we can train this  is with a club bell or an indian club  there's tons of movements that are  fantastic for training in the transverse  plane one of my favorite being the atlas  swing and here you're going to take the  club on one side you're going to swing  it horizontally across your body  like so  as you do this you're transitioning the  weight from one foot to the other  and you're driving through the hips  as much  as you're driving through the core  so those movements are great if you want  to build ballistic and explosive  strength in the transverse plane but you  also want to build strength in the  transverse plane so you want to find  something where you're going to feel  resistance all the way through the  movement and we can load up the amount  of resistance a lot higher and for this  some brilliant movements you can use are  the wood chopper and the cable punch out  i'm doing it here with bands it's even  more effective with a cable because you  have a slightly more linear strength  curve but either way this challenges you  to turn through the core and the hips  but at the same time you're pulling  against a decent amount of resistance to  really build strength here  and here i'm training for a wide range  of motion and i can really add as much  resistance  as i want  and so the cable or band punch out is  basically like a band or cable press but  the difference is that you're using a  staggered stance and then you're going  to again twist  through the hips at the same time as  extending the fist obviously this isn't  going to build that looseness and the  snapping power you want for a punch but  it is going to build that stable base of  strength from which to draw upon through  that range of motion and focusing on all  those muscles and even if you're not a  fighter once again you need strength  here this is a great way to build it and  to add  some real resistance there as well  so there's some exercises and some ideas  for training your rotational strength  and the key here is to use a combination  of these things as usual so you want to  train anti-rotation you want to train  through a wide range of motion you want  to train just the core on its own you  want to train the core with the hips you  want strength you want ballistic  strength and you want explosiveness if  you do all that you'll have a fantastic  core it's extremely powerful  thank you so much for watching this one  guys let me know in the comments down  below what you do to train your core a  huge thank you to grant stevens for  coming and filming again today be sure  to check out his channel if you want  martial arts and training tips i'll  leave a link to that description down  below as usual if you like training that  focuses not just on building strength  and aesthetics but all around mobility  and performance even brain training then  check out my ebook and training program  super functional training there's a  discount on and a link in the  description down below thank you so much  for watching this one guys please like  and share it around if you would that  helps me out immensely and i'll see you  next time thanks for watching  and bye for now  i

